{"id":"missing-value-imputation","t":"Column-wise missing value imputation","tags":["Data Processing","Feature Engineering"],"d":"Easy","rt":"numpy","fn":"impute","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"Missing values are encoded as `nan`. Write `impute(X_train, X_test, strategy)` that learns one fill value per column from `X_train` and uses it to replace the `nan`s in **both** arrays.\n\n## Strategies\n| `strategy` | fill value for a column |\n| --- | --- |\n| `'mean'` | mean of the column's non-missing training values |\n| `'median'` | median of the non-missing training values (average of the two middle values when their count is even) |\n| `'most_frequent'` | the most common non-missing training value; ties go to the **smallest** value |\n\nIf a column has **no** non-missing training values, its fill value is `0.0`.\n\n## Input / output\n- `X_train`: float `(n, d)`; `X_test`: float `(m, d)`; either may contain `nan` anywhere.\n- Return `(train_filled, test_filled, fill)`: float arrays `(n, d)`, `(m, d)` and `(d,)`. Don't modify the inputs.\n- Fill values come from the training data only, never from `X_test`.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef impute(X_train, X_test, strategy):\n    \"\"\"Fill nan per column using statistics of X_train. Return (train_filled, test_filled, fill_values).\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["`np.nan` is not equal to itself, so `X == np.nan` finds nothing. Use `np.isnan` to build the mask.","For each column, drop the missing entries first, then compute the statistic on what's left. `np.nanmean`/`np.nanmedian` exist, but they warn and return `nan` on an all-missing column.","Fill with fancy indexing: `rows, cols = np.nonzero(np.isnan(A)); A[rows, cols] = fill[cols]`. For the mode, `np.unique(..., return_counts=True)` sorts the values, so `argmax` breaks ties toward the smallest."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef impute(X_train, X_test, strategy):\n    X_train, X_test = np.array(X_train, dtype=float), np.array(X_test, dtype=float)   # copies\n    fill = np.zeros(X_train.shape[1])\n    for j in range(X_train.shape[1]):\n        col = X_train[:, j][~np.isnan(X_train[:, j])]\n        if len(col) == 0:\n            continue                                         # nothing observed: keep 0.0\n        if strategy == 'mean':\n            fill[j] = col.mean()\n        elif strategy == 'median':\n            fill[j] = np.median(col)\n        else:\n            vals, counts = np.unique(col, return_counts=True)\n            fill[j] = vals[counts.argmax()]                  # vals ascending: first max is the smallest value\n    for A in (X_train, X_test):\n        rows, cols = np.nonzero(np.isnan(A))\n        A[rows, cols] = fill[cols]\n    return X_train, X_test, fill\n","ex_md":"## Why fit on train only\nThe fill value is a learned parameter, just like a scaler's mean. Computing it on the test set (or on train + test) leaks information about the evaluation data into the pipeline and makes offline metrics optimistic. In production there's no \"test set\" to peek at anyway: new rows are filled with the training statistic.\n\n## Choosing a strategy\n- **Mean** keeps the column mean unchanged but shrinks its variance and is pulled by outliers (test 6 has $\\pm 10^9$).\n- **Median** is robust to outliers and skew.\n- **Most frequent** works for discrete or categorical-coded columns.\nAll three ignore relationships between features. k-NN or iterative (regression-based) imputation use them, at a higher cost.\n\n## Complexity\n$O(nd)$ for mean and mask work; median and mode add a sort per column, $O(n\\log n)$ each.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Checking `x == np.nan` or `x is np.nan`.\n- Using `np.nanmean` on an all-`nan` column and propagating `nan` into the output (test 4).\n- Mutating the caller's arrays in place.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Why add a binary \"was missing\" indicator feature, and when does missingness itself carry signal (missing not at random)?\n- How do gradient-boosted tree libraries handle `nan` without imputation? (Each split learns a default direction for missing values.)","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[ 1., nan],\n       [ 3.,  4.],\n       [nan,  8.]])\narray([[nan, nan]])\n'mean'","out":"(array([[1., 6.],\n       [3., 4.],\n       [2., 8.]]), array([[2., 6.]]), array([2., 6.]))"},{"in":"array([[ 1.,  2.],\n       [nan,  2.],\n       [ 7.,  5.],\n       [ 4., nan]])\narray([[nan,  1.]])\n'median'","out":"(array([[1., 2.],\n       [4., 2.],\n       [7., 5.],\n       [4., 2.]]), array([[4., 1.]]), array([4., 2.]))"}],"lessons":[{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/ai-research-track-web/machine-learning-operations-mlops/data-validation-transformation/","t":"Data Validation & Transformation","c":"AI Research"},{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/machine-learning/feature-engineering/missing-data/","t":"Missing data","c":"Machine Learning"},{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/ml-toolkit/numpy/reductions-and-the-axis-argument/","t":"Reductions and the axis argument","c":"ML Toolkit"}],"related":["feature-scaling","categorical-encoding"],"ntests":8,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, {\"__f__\": \"nan\"}], [3.0, 4.0], [{\"__f__\": \"nan\"}, 8.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[{\"__f__\": \"nan\"}, {\"__f__\": \"nan\"}]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, \"mean\"], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 6.0], [3.0, 4.0], [2.0, 8.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[2.0, 6.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [2.0, 6.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 2.0], [{\"__f__\": \"nan\"}, 2.0], [7.0, 5.0], [4.0, {\"__f__\": \"nan\"}]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[{\"__f__\": \"nan\"}, 1.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, \"median\"], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 2.0], [4.0, 2.0], [7.0, 5.0], [4.0, 2.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[4.0, 1.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [4.0, 2.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0], [2.0], [2.0], [1.0], [{\"_